  "vulnerability_information": "At https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/curl-8_21_0/scripts/wcurl#L198, `get_url_filename()` protects percent-encoded `%2F` and `%5C`, but leaves a literal backslash unchanged before it becomes the automatic output filename.\n\nOn Windows, run `wcurl.bat \"http://127.0.0.1:PORT/sub\\..\\..\\escaped.txt\"` from `sandbox\\inside` while a local server returns a marker; curl exits 0 and creates `sandbox\\escaped.txt` outside the working directory.\n\nI reproduced this twice on native Windows 10 build 19045 using the current official curl 8.21.0_7 x64 archive, including its shipped `wcurl.bat`, `wcurl`, and `curl.exe`.\n\nThe control URL ending `sub%5C..%5C..%5Cescaped.txt` stays inside the working directory, so the published CVE-2025-11563 percent-encoding repair works while the literal-backslash bypass remains.\n\nTo rerun it, extract the official archive, obtain `busybox64u.exe` from https://github.com/rmyorston/busybox-w32, then run `python3 02-poc-wcurl-literal-backslash.py --curl-bin ./curl-8.21.0_7-win64-mingw/bin --busybox ./busybox64u.exe --work-dir ./poc-output`.\n\nI used AI to assist the source audit and drafting, and personally reran the release proof, control, current-tree check, and all 28 upstream tests.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker-controlled URL can make wcurl create a new attacker-controlled file outside the directory chosen by a Windows user, subject to that user's filesystem permissions and the target not already existing; modern curl's `--no-clobber` prevents overwriting an existing target.",
